            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "verify=False disables certificate verification, so a network attacker can present any certificate and read or alter the traffic."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "Python call that disables TLS certificate verification via verify=False.\n\n**Why it matters.** verify=False disables certificate verification, so a network attacker can present any certificate and read or alter the traffic.\n\n**How to verify.** Restore verification (verify=True or a trusted CA bundle); if a self-signed host is truly required, pin its certificate instead of disabling verification.\n\nA RigorFoundry result is an audit candidate for evidence review, never a defect verdict; only a validated review record decides it.",
                "text": "Python call that disables TLS certificate verification via verify=False.\n\nWhy it matters: verify=False disables certificate verification, so a network attacker can present any certificate and read or alter the traffic.\nHow to verify: Restore verification (verify=True or a trusted CA bundle); if a self-signed host is truly required, pin its certificate instead of disabling verification."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/anulum/RIGOR-FOUNDRY/blob/main/docs/rule-catalogue.md#as012-disabled-tls-verification",
              "id": "AS012-disabled-tls-verification",
              "name": "AS012-disabled-tls-verification",
              "properties": {
                "category": "application-security",
                "introduced": "rigor-foundry/1.7.0",
                "tags": [
                  "rigor-foundry",
                  "application-security"
                ]
              },
              "relationships": [
                {
                  "kinds": [
                    "subset"
                  ],
                  "target": {
                    "id": "application-security",
                    "index": 4,
                    "toolComponent": {
                      "index": 0,
                      "name": "RigorFoundry rule categories"
                    }
                  }
                }
              ],
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Python call that disables TLS certificate verification via verify=False."
              }
            },
            {
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "ssl._create_unverified_context returns a context that accepts any certificate, so the peer is never authenticated."
              },
              "help": {
                "markdown": "Python use of ssl._create_unverified_context, which authenticates no peer.\n\n**Why it matters.** ssl._create_unverified_context returns a context that accepts any certificate, so the peer is never authenticated.\n\n**How to verify.** Use ssl.create_default_context, or a context with an explicit trusted CA, so the peer certificate is verified.\n\nA RigorFoundry result is an audit candidate for evidence review, never a defect verdict; only a validated review record decides it.",
                "text": "Python use of ssl._create_unverified_context, which authenticates no peer.\n\nWhy it matters: ssl._create_unverified_context returns a context that accepts any certificate, so the peer is never authenticated.\nHow to verify: Use ssl.create_default_context, or a context with an explicit trusted CA, so the peer certificate is verified."
              },
              "helpUri": "https://github.com/anulum/RIGOR-FOUNDRY/blob/main/docs/rule-catalogue.md#as013-insecure-ssl-context",
              "id": "AS013-insecure-ssl-context",
